ughter was a multiple sports player but began private tennis lessons the summer of her freshman year. She was a natural but tennis is not a sport you just “pick up” and are fabulous at it. The hardest one she has played, she says. She quit her other sports, except goalie in soccer her Sophomore year. After that, she dropped everything else to focus because she was already behind. Traveled 1:40 hrs one way 2-3 days a week for lessons. After finishing her last senior year at State and recuperating from a bone bruise, she hit it hard and that’s when we started the recruiting process. She was not a D1 player. D2 would have been a stretch though several colleges were interested in her. NAIA was our goal. She visited 3 NAIA schools in the surrounding states and two D3. D3 was more $$$ and of course there is no scholarship money. Her gpa was really good and her ACT was good but not 32+ good. Merit scholarships would help but still more $. The school she is going to in the fall offered a ￼scholarship and she negotiated with them. It is an amazing program that seems to want to continue helping her develop, where some of the other schools offered her, just wanted her to play. I know this is a lot but I hope it helps!","createdAt":"2026-06-14T23:26:49.000Z","replyCount":2,"reactionCount":2,"repliesCursor":"","author":{"name":"Dawn Franks Harris","platformUserId":"1378524435","shortName":"Dawn"}},{"id":"Y29tbWVudDozMDM2NjY5NTkzMTczNDgzXzMwMzY2ODM3MDY1MDU0MDU=","text":"No recruiting company needed! What do you mean by hard-headed? That may help us figure out how to help. Please be sure he actually wants to play in college and is willing to put in the time to send emails, fill our recruiting forms, get on calls with coaches, go on visits, etc.","createdAt":"2026-06-14T22:43:21.000Z","replyCount":0,"reactionCount":2,"repliesCursor":"","author":{"name":"Olga Norstrom","platformUserId":"pfbid0c6zkX6kSEES16xjB699d1BgCNi7dHq29sqfCn2QnhsZGjWDongWruVdoUqFbJx7Al","shortName":"Olga"}},{"i